encrypting Kubernetes secrets

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.168
with Cloud KMS.

00:01:53.168 --> 00:01:54.710
This is actually
really, really cool.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:56.590
So if you used
Kubernetes, Kubernetes

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:59.200
has a thing called Secrets,
where you can store data in it,

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.180
especially things like
passwords and whatnot.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.370
But by default, the
Kubernetes Secrets

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.380
are stored in plain
text, which if you

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.850
want to put really secret
stuff in there, is not ideal.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.720
But there is a new
thing in GKE, which

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.850
is application-layer
secret encryption, that

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.200
is in beta right now, that
you can take advantage of.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.830
It's pretty
straightforward to set up.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:18.730
All you really need to
do is specify the key

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:20.740
that you want to
use inside Cloud KMS

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.410
to manage your secrets, and
pretty much you're good to go.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:24.430
If you have a look
at the article,

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.597
it shows you a little Getting
Started Guide, as well

00:02:26.597 --> 00:02:28.708
as links to some documentation
to get you going.

00:02:28.708 --> 00:02:29.500
