00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:19.890
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: Yeah, so as
you can imagine from the name,

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.820
we're all about voice, and
enterprise specifically.

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:25.370
So if you think, really,
about how we use voice

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:28.100
in our day-to-day,
especially at work,

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:30.510
there are almost
three big categories.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.960
One of them is
actions that you take.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:36.660
So imagine, for example,
if you have an assistant,

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:38.702
like Alexa or Google Assistant.

00:05:38.702 --> 00:05:40.160
And then you talk
to the assistant,

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.920
and it executes the actions for
you, like turn on the lights

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:43.940
and so on.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:46.460
The big category for
us is that workflow

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:47.960
integrations, that
you actually want

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:50.000
to automate a lot of actions.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.570
And enterprise-- you can
imagine you walk to a meeting,

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:54.600
and you're kind of lost.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.300
You don't know
what the agenda is,

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.892
what is going to happen
after the meeting is done.

00:05:58.892 --> 00:06:00.350
And we want to
automate the process

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:05.010
of listing other outcomes of the
meeting in a very concise way,

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:06.920
and converting
those into actions.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:08.800
So you can say,
like our assistant

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:10.910
is Eva, what's the stock
price of Google today?

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:12.410
Create a task for
me to follow up

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:14.750
with IT regarding
security policies.

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.600
And this becomes an
actual action or task

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:19.600
in your task management
system, like Trello

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:21.540
or your system of choice.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:23.660
And then the other big
category is information.

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:25.220
So you are in a
meeting, and you want

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:27.440
to capture or query
some information

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:28.530
from another system.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:33.350
So for example, you can ask
Eva, OK, Eva, show networks

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:34.150
or social media.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:35.650
MARK MANDEL: I'm
not doing that now.

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:36.210
I promise.

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:36.840
GABI FERRARA: No,
nobody does that.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.660
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: So
focusing on the conversations

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:40.035
and focusing on
the people there.

00:06:40.035 --> 00:06:42.620
So that's the biggest
value add, I think.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:45.560
When you're in a meeting, you're
very focused, you're present,

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:48.140
and you have really
high attention AQ--

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.720
so attention quotient, I think.

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:52.520
So that's the word
I was looking for.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:55.070
And you want to be focused on
the conversation and the people

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:55.765
around you.

00:06:55.765 --> 00:06:57.140
And Eva would
basically take care

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:59.887
of taking notes when
you tell the assistant,

00:06:59.887 --> 00:07:01.970
this is an important moment,
and this is an action

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:03.548
I want to be following up with.

00:07:03.548 --> 00:07:05.090
MARK MANDEL: That
sounds really cool.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:06.945
You've also got
integrations with a bunch

00:07:06.945 --> 00:07:07.820
of different systems.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:10.143
You mentioned action items,
and actually doing things.

00:07:10.143 --> 00:07:11.810
Can you tell us, what
does that actually

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:12.935
look like in my day-to-day?

00:07:12.935 --> 00:07:15.080
Am I sending
notifications via email?

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:16.760
Or what are the cool
things I can do?

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:17.070
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: Right.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:18.487
So a lot of these
systems actually

00:07:18.487 --> 00:07:23.870
allow you to send them emails
to create something in them.

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:26.240
You can, for example,
create a card in Trello,

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:29.720
or create a task in
Jira, or a ticket.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:31.748
And we plug in
into a lot of them.

00:07:31.748 --> 00:07:33.290
So you can actually
go to our website

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:36.140
and explore other
systems we plug into.

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:39.650
And we configure it easily
with a single email address.

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:41.730
And Eva will send
anything that you

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:44.870
want capture into these
systems, and basically starting

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:48.080
that workflow, kicking off
that action, which is maybe

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:50.870
creating a note, or creating a
task, and so on and so forth.

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:53.780
If you imagine you have
your notepad and your pen,

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:55.940
and you walk to a meeting,
you can actually talk,

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:58.230
and you can tell Eva
that this is important,

00:07:58.230 --> 00:07:59.970
and this is what I
wanted to capture.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:03.800
Instead of like scribbling, and
maybe losing a few minutes here

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:05.598
and there of that
meeting you could

00:08:05.598 --> 00:08:07.640
have spent focusing more
on the people around you

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:09.240
and the conversation, for sure.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:09.510
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:11.240
So yeah, you're the
chief architect,

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:12.438
and this is the GC podcast.

00:08:12.438 --> 00:08:14.480
I want to get into the
