00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:33.770
Of course we understand
how GKE works,

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:36.590
and we know how to tweak
it to-- our systems

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:38.970
are very complex as well.

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:41.540
But it takes all of
complexity away for sure.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:44.870
So Compute Engine,
GKE, Stackdriver,

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:48.350
all of these tools, also the
storage systems and whatnot

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:51.860
are really great for
any startup to go to GCP

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:54.110
and start working on
growing their business

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:57.420
and not worrying about
managing services like that.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:00.470
GABI FERRARA: So with all of
this tooling that you use,

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:02.840
sometimes you face
some challenges

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:04.842
because you have
a learning curve.

00:10:04.842 --> 00:10:06.550
You need to learn how
to use Stackdriver.

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:09.890
I need to learn how to use
this or how to use that.

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:14.270
How does this affect the
development and the challenges

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:18.422
that you ended up facing
for your product on Voicea?

00:10:18.422 --> 00:10:20.130
MOHAMED EL-GEISH:
That's a good question.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:22.900
So there are layers
of abstractions

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:27.010
that might isolate you from
the underlying problems

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:29.262
that a lot of
distributive systems face.

00:10:29.262 --> 00:10:30.970
So back in the day
when you were building

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:32.890
your own setup,
your own cluster,

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:36.760
you had to worry about where
I'm going to get my machines.

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:40.030
So if I have to buy
or provision hardware,

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:41.648
I have to put it
in a data center,

00:10:41.648 --> 00:10:43.690
I actually have to worry
about the correct space,

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:45.065
I have to worry
about networking,

00:10:45.065 --> 00:10:47.050
I have to worry about
physical security.

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:49.143
I have to worry about
a lot of things.

00:10:49.143 --> 00:10:50.560
And then after all
this, you still

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:53.590
have to worry about
the actual systems that

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:57.040
are running the databases
and file storage,

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:01.060
and backing up systems
redundancy, high availability.

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:02.890
And on top of all of
this, your application

00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:04.780
has to be up and
running, and available,

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:08.240
and performance, and all
these nice characteristics.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.110
So if you think about
the balance between doing

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:13.210
all of the above versus
learning a framework

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:15.190
or learning something
that's, I think,

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:17.950
relatively simpler, like
how to work with Kubernetes

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:19.570
or how to work
with Stackdriver, I

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:22.210
think we're in a much,
much better place today.

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:25.330
It of course can improve,
meaning that there are actually

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:28.690
some startups and some other
companies whose entire business

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:31.910
model is basically
simplifying this even more.

00:11:31.910 --> 00:11:34.000
But I think it's the
right balance for what

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:37.720
I would call understanding
the complexity underneath,

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:40.210
or basically peeling
the layers of the onion.

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:42.010
If you're working on
an operating system,

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:44.710
you need to understand a
little bit of the foundations

00:11:44.710 --> 00:11:48.730
of this operating system, how it
schedules tasks, how it manages

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:51.280
files, and so on and so forth.

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:53.470
In the world we
live in, you have

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:56.080
to understand the data center
operating system, which

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:59.590
is in our case Kubernetes,
like how it schedules Docker

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:03.190
containers, and what happens
when some conditions aren't

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:05.650
met, and you might
be in the risk

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:07.580
of losing your data or not.

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:09.262
So there are of
course some constructs

00:12:09.262 --> 00:12:10.220
you have to understand.

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:12.100
But for the most
part, I think we're

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:15.910
in a much, much, much
better place than before.

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:18.730
And that allows us to
move faster and focus

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:23.770
on our own application
concerns, versus learning way

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:25.570
deeper things in the stack.

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:29.620
And of course, the thing that
I would emphasize in this

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:31.690
is, the more you know,
the better you are.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:32.740
Knowledge is power.

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:35.380
And if you understand
Kubernetes very well

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:37.960
and if you understand
how Stackdriver works,

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:40.910
you're going to be able to
perform faster and move faster.

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:41.660
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:44.218
I'd love to hear more about
the flow of information,

00:12:44.218 --> 00:12:45.010
too, in the system.

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:46.510
Because I'm guessing
there's more to it

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:47.650
than just Kubernetes, right?

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:49.580
I'm guessing there's some
audio coming in there,

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:50.955
and doing some
processing, maybe.

00:12:50.955 --> 00:12:52.910
And then are you running
custom ML models,

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:55.167
you putting other tools in it?

00:12:55.167 --> 00:12:56.500
What's that flow of information?

00:12:56.500 --> 00:12:58.210
How is that all
getting processed?

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:00.127
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: Yeah,
it's a great question.

00:13:00.127 --> 00:13:02.450
So our basic input is audio.

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:05.260
And there's some metadata
that are associated

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:06.940
with such audio recordings.

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:09.710
So we join the meeting, and
we record what's happening.

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:11.830
We also respond to commands.

00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:14.140
And that's technology
in machine learning

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:15.820
known as keyword spotting.

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:18.970
So we run this in a
very real-time fashion,

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:22.420
that you want to respond right
away to the user's commands

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:24.110
and execute them.

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:26.110
So there's a really
good separation

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:30.520
here between what we can
classify as real-time, versus

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:33.670
nearline, versus
offline processing

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:37.060
of audio and other
metadata we consume.

00:13:37.060 --> 00:13:39.820
And we depend
heavily on Kubernetes

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:43.630
to actually schedule all
this workload for us.

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:45.430
So the flow of the
data is basically

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:48.190
the audio is being
captured real-time,

00:13:48.190 --> 00:13:50.500
and we're responding to
commands using machine learning

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:51.880
algorithms in real-time.

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:54.460
And we do one pass
on the transcript,

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:57.260
and then we capture
the audio in storage,

00:13:57.260 --> 00:14:00.040
and then we do another
multi-passes on that.

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:03.680
And we produce the transcript
for the user at the end.

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:07.780
So as you can see, the
biggest focus for us

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:10.480
is actually how to be always
available, capture the audio,

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:11.530
and not drop it.

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:15.220
And also always be performance
with really low latency

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:16.850
to respond to these commands.

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:19.090
So that's when you're having
this experience, which

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:23.410
is fairly unique, you have
a very powerful computer

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:25.420
or personal assistant
on the phone with you

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:26.710
on the conference line.

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:28.660
So we're talking
now on this line,

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:30.930
and we can actually
invite Eva, and Eva

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:33.820
will be capturing this and
responding to my commands.

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:37.030
I'm telling Eva, highlight this,
or this is an important note,

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:41.240
or take an action as an action
item, and so on and so forth.

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:43.990
So all of these have
to be very performant

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:45.790
and have very low latency.

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:49.240
So we understand the
complexities of, like,

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:52.640
how to route audio
and RTP, or if I'm

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:56.200
a protocol on top of
Kubernetes, and we

00:14:56.200 --> 00:15:01.105
have certain requirements around
CPU utilization and memory

00:15:01.105 --> 00:15:04.600
utilization for these systems
that are processing audio

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:07.150
in real-time, which is very
important to understand--

00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:09.820
the constraints of the
system when you're using

00:15:09.820 --> 00:15:11.720
one scheduler versus the other.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:13.390
And we're really
happy with the balance

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:17.480
we have today, in terms of how
to separate these workloads

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:19.690
in different note pools,
and how to schedule them all

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:22.490
to scaling so that
we are not always

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:23.960
over-provisioning the machines.

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:28.340
So our workload varies
according to business hours

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:29.400
in different locations.

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:31.030
So we can actually
kind of get an idea

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:33.280
of when we're going to need
more machines versus we're

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:36.200
going to need fewer machines,
and so on and so forth,

00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:37.232
for each system.

00:15:37.232 --> 00:15:38.690
So it's a very
challenging problem.

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:41.340
But Kubernetes is helping
us manage that very well.

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:42.590
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, that's fun.

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:44.200
What are you building
your ML models in?

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:46.550
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: Oh, so that's
a great question about machine

00:15:46.550 --> 00:15:48.560
learning in general,
and the philosophy we're

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:52.140
taking in building systems.

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:54.950
So as a very fresh
or early startup,

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:57.140
when we started a
couple of years ago,

00:15:57.140 --> 00:16:00.830
we had this list of principles
about software engineering

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:05.030
in general, and specifically
about AI and machine learning,

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:08.300
in which we set a set
of guiding principles

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:12.300
for basically coming up with
this decision-making framework.

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:14.130
So when you ask somebody
in the team, hey,

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:16.047
what are you going to
use to build this model,

00:16:16.047 --> 00:16:19.950
there should be a very easy list
of criteria to make a decision.

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:23.240
So for example, as a company
that's centered around voice,

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:26.240
and the voice of our users is
very important us to protect,

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:28.530
security is always a
top priority for us.

00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:31.250
So a lot of these systems
in machine learning

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:33.530
are really good about security.

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:35.790
We're serving them
through Kubernetes.

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:37.580
So the training part
of machine learning

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:39.435
happens, of course, offline.

00:16:39.435 --> 00:16:41.060
But if you're serving
something online,

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:42.950
we're protecting the
data, we're making sure

00:16:42.950 --> 00:16:45.840
that everything is
secure encrypted,

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:49.040
then you talk about other
frameworks characteristics that

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:52.490
meet the other set of priorities
for us, or guiding principles.

00:16:52.490 --> 00:16:54.200
So you talk about
speed of iteration.

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:57.260
And speed of iteration
or velocity of iteration

00:16:57.260 --> 00:16:59.810
is extremely important
in machine learning.

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:03.560
Machine learning, by definition,
or the way it's done,

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:05.005
is very iterative.

00:17:05.005 --> 00:17:06.380
It's extremely
iterative, meaning

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:10.560
that you start with some idea,
and you build some hypotheses,

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:13.369
and then you say, this
would work on paper.

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:16.040
And you actually go and
deploy it in the wild,

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:18.240
and you get some feedback,
and you say, OK, I'm

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:19.490
going to have to improve this.

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:22.520
And now you collect more data,
and you keep iterating on it.

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:24.710
So there are a lot
of frameworks today,

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.480
like TensorFlow and
others, that make

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:28.410
this process extremely easy.

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:32.630
We have also some flexibility
around what framework we use,

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:34.760
thanks to a new
framework called Onyx,

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:38.780
that allows you to build models
using multiple frameworks.

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:40.790
And at the end
you can serve them

00:17:40.790 --> 00:17:44.540
using one of them or a single
engine that you choose,

00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:45.900
and you can serve from that.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:47.540
So we have some
flexibility and freedom

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:50.600
that we allow folks on
the team to actually use

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:53.875
multiple frameworks, including
on the cloud if they wish.

00:17:53.875 --> 00:17:55.250
But at the end of
the day, we try

00:17:55.250 --> 00:17:58.070
to standardize the serving
mechanism so that you always

00:17:58.070 --> 00:17:59.270
serve on Kubernetes.

00:17:59.270 --> 00:18:00.230
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:01.813
GABI FERRARA: So
with all this machine

00:18:01.813 --> 00:18:04.440
learning that you do
with different models,

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:08.750
how do you see you serving
the enterprise world,

00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:11.630
where everyone has
a different demand,

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:15.000
everyone wants a piece of
you in a different way?

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:17.372
How do you see serving
the enterprise world?

00:18:17.372 --> 00:18:18.830
MOHAMED EL-GEISH:
Yeah, so there is

00:18:18.830 --> 00:18:23.650
this concept of well-posed
machine learning problems

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:27.080
in the sense that, these kind
of problems, when you're trying

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:29.840
to solve them, you
build up something

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:33.710
and you put it out there, and
users have some experiences

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:37.040
with them, and then
to use the data that

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:40.610
come from the users or the
results of their output

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:42.890
as feedback so that
they can learn more.

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:44.450
And this is where
we are, actually.

00:18:44.450 --> 00:18:47.070
It's a virtuous
data network effect,

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:50.023
meaning that if you
are using Voicea today,

00:18:50.023 --> 00:18:51.440
and let's say
you're in a meeting,

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:55.030
and then Eva transcribes
something or creates

00:18:55.030 --> 00:19:00.110
a task for you, and there is a
very jargony word that you use

00:19:00.110 --> 00:19:03.055
that Eva did not recognize,
you can go to our interface,

00:19:03.055 --> 00:19:06.320
you can fix it there, and
then we will learn from that,

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:08.780
so that the next time
we would actually

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:12.590
make our best to mitigate
that or fix it for you.

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:16.010
And then the more users we
have from the same industry,

00:19:16.010 --> 00:19:18.980
we can have a more
explicit effort

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:22.010
to actually prepare
the words that

00:19:22.010 --> 00:19:26.000
are pertaining to that
industry or that domain first,

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:28.250
before you actually
use a system,

00:19:28.250 --> 00:19:30.840
or as you can see
the data effect,

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:32.900
the more you use it, the
more it learns from you.

00:19:32.900 --> 00:19:36.120
And then it becomes
even more accurate.

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:38.070
So that's really a good
place to be at what

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:40.093
are your problem's well-posed.

00:19:40.093 --> 00:19:41.510
MARK MANDEL: Ooh,
but what happens

00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:43.250
if you have the
same term being used

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:45.172
across different industries
in different ways?

00:19:45.172 --> 00:19:46.130
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: Yeah.

00:19:46.130 --> 00:19:48.290
That's when, actually,
you have to collect

00:19:48.290 --> 00:19:50.630
some metadata about
who's talking,

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:51.980
and it's more personalized.

00:19:51.980 --> 00:19:54.080
And then you can
disambiguate between the two.

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:54.830
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:19:54.830 --> 00:19:56.090
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: So they
might sound the same,

00:19:56.090 --> 00:19:58.700
but they actually might
be spelled differently.

00:19:58.700 --> 00:20:00.520
But because you know
who's talking first,

00:20:00.520 --> 00:20:02.780
like I know your
email address, I

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:04.580
know some data
about the speaker,

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:06.163
so it's more
personalized towards you.

00:20:06.163 --> 00:20:07.580
MARK MANDEL: So
you basically need

00:20:07.580 --> 00:20:09.010
the contextual
information, right?

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:10.010
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: Right.

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:12.340
And we get that because you
actually sign up with us,

00:20:12.340 --> 00:20:14.970
we know who you
are, and we kind of

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:18.685
let Eva learn from your
idiosyncracies, basically.

00:20:18.685 --> 00:20:20.810
GABI FERRARA: So that's
how you learn accents, too?

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:23.360
Because I have problems
of some assistants,

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:28.130
because when I say some words,
they don't get what I'm saying.

00:20:28.130 --> 00:20:30.193
And accent is really
hard, you know?

00:20:30.193 --> 00:20:32.360
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: Yeah,
that's part of the solution.

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:32.780
Yeah.

00:20:32.780 --> 00:20:33.440
GABI FERRARA: Nice.

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:34.773
MARK MANDEL: That's pretty cool.

00:20:34.773 --> 00:20:37.540
So obviously you're doing
a lot of voice stuff.

00:20:37.540 --> 00:20:38.790
Is this is just the beginning?

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:39.690
Do you plan on doing more?

00:20:39.690 --> 00:20:41.732
Where do you see the voice
interaction experience

00:20:41.732 --> 00:20:42.315
kind of going?

00:20:42.315 --> 00:20:44.065
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: I
would really love if I

00:20:44.065 --> 00:20:45.290
could have demoed our system.

00:20:45.290 --> 00:20:48.950
Because we are also introducing
some video elements,

00:20:48.950 --> 00:20:51.560
specifically when it's
a presentation or Slides

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:53.930
that you're showing inside
the context of a meeting.

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:55.910
It's actually very
interesting to see

00:20:55.910 --> 00:20:57.830
the interaction
between the voice

00:20:57.830 --> 00:21:00.740
and the playback of the
slides, and the highlights

00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:03.200
of the meeting, the
minutes that we captured.

00:21:03.200 --> 00:21:05.420
It's a very rich
experience, and we're also

00:21:05.420 --> 00:21:08.330
looking for something
that we can capture--

00:21:08.330 --> 00:21:10.047
whether it's audio or video--

00:21:10.047 --> 00:21:11.630
to get more context
about the meeting,

00:21:11.630 --> 00:21:14.990
and give it to you in a
consumable format that

00:21:14.990 --> 00:21:18.140
is easy to integrate
with your workflow.

00:21:18.140 --> 00:21:20.480
So today, we actually
also would have

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:23.090
some playback of the slides
or the video you had.

00:21:23.090 --> 00:21:26.300
GABI FERRARA: What is the most
interesting voice application

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:29.530
you created at the
Voicea, the most

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:32.840
like challenging and
interesting thing

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:34.743
you did with that technology?

00:21:34.743 --> 00:21:37.160
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: I have to
think a little bit about this.

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:38.510
There are a lot of
challenges in building

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:39.440
a startup from scratch--

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:39.860
GABI FERRARA: [LAUGHS]

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:41.277
MOHAMED EL-GEISH:
--and definitely

00:21:41.277 --> 00:21:44.390
when it deals with
non-deterministic input

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:45.860
like speech.

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:48.080
So we have a lot of
machine learning domains

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:52.160
that are easier than speech
or perceptual knowledge

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:53.090
acquisition.

00:21:53.090 --> 00:21:54.890
So for example,
if you're thinking

00:21:54.890 --> 00:21:56.990
of recommendation systems,
which is something

00:21:56.990 --> 00:21:59.270
I used to do at my
previous life at LinkedIn,

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:02.930
I think you have more leeway
or you have more freedom

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:05.910
in showing the user
commendations and so on, on,

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:07.560
for example, the news feed.

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:11.030
But when you're dealing with
human perceptual tasks--

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:14.300
like I heard something
and I need to make sure

00:22:14.300 --> 00:22:16.160
that this is the
transcript for it,

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:17.630
it's a bit more challenging.

00:22:17.630 --> 00:22:20.690
I want to say, for sure, one
of the most challenging ones

00:22:20.690 --> 00:22:23.870
is keyword spotting, and
responding to commands.

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:27.140
It's almost a magical moment
when you walk into a meeting,

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:30.140
and then can talk to
the conference line,

00:22:30.140 --> 00:22:31.700
and you get stuff done.

00:22:31.700 --> 00:22:35.410
That's for sure a very
challenging process

00:22:35.410 --> 00:22:36.698
and challenging problem.

00:22:36.698 --> 00:22:39.240
Because you need to understand
the intent of what the user is

00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:42.230
saying, you need to understand
where this information should

00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:45.200
flow, and you need
to convert that data

00:22:45.200 --> 00:22:47.690
into an actionable outcome.

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:49.550
The combination of
these problems, I think,

00:22:49.550 --> 00:22:52.070
is the most challenging and
most interesting, for sure.

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:52.360
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:54.777
And what's been the-- on the
customer side, though, what's

00:22:54.777 --> 00:22:56.880
been the most interesting
integration you've seen?

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:57.920
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: Oh,
definitely integrations

00:22:57.920 --> 00:22:59.360
with task management systems.

00:22:59.360 --> 00:23:02.180
Because a lot of people
like to keep track of what

00:23:02.180 --> 00:23:05.330
they're doing, and they
think of meetings as a place

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:08.810
where they get work done and a
place where you can coordinate

00:23:08.810 --> 00:23:10.260
and discuss ideas.

00:23:10.260 --> 00:23:13.550
And then they come out with
some maybe handwritten notes,

00:23:13.550 --> 00:23:15.770
or maybe some cryptic
notes that they

00:23:15.770 --> 00:23:17.780
captured during the
meeting because they really

00:23:17.780 --> 00:23:18.572
want to be engaged.

00:23:18.572 --> 00:23:20.640
And they just type a few
words here and there.

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:23.690
But now they can just
get out of the experience

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:29.420
with a very nicely
fleshed-out list of outcomes

00:23:29.420 --> 00:23:33.050
that they just input
using the voice.

00:23:33.050 --> 00:23:36.140
I think this is actually
where voice shines.

00:23:36.140 --> 00:23:39.630
It's the natural way of
communicating with computers.

00:23:39.630 --> 00:23:42.740
So anybody who's interested
in Human-Computer Interaction,

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:45.930
or HCI, they can tell you
that, in the beginning,

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:46.910
we had the terminal.

00:23:46.910 --> 00:23:50.390
We had just words
and the command line,

00:23:50.390 --> 00:23:53.540
and a very dull
way of interfacing,

00:23:53.540 --> 00:23:55.910
because that was the
limitation of that era.

00:23:55.910 --> 00:23:57.470
And then we advanced
a little bit

00:23:57.470 --> 00:24:00.380
through the Graphical
User Interface, or GUI.

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:03.350
And that, also, because we
couldn't do better than that.

00:24:03.350 --> 00:24:06.450
If you maybe saw some of
the early systems that

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:10.400
used voice or other natural
UI methods of input,

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:12.050
they weren't that
good back in the day.

00:24:12.050 --> 00:24:15.560
So we had to resort to
the mouse or the pointer,

00:24:15.560 --> 00:24:18.170
and then some icons and whatnot.

00:24:18.170 --> 00:24:21.890
But now, with the richness of
machine learning techniques

00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:24.810
and deep learning, and all the
advances we had in the past,

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:26.750
I would say, five
to six years, it

00:24:26.750 --> 00:24:28.820
became possible to
actually walk into a room,

00:24:28.820 --> 00:24:31.760
and have a $20 device
that talks to you.

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:35.480
And you can do things with
it, which is, in my opinion,

00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:38.930
it's just a revolution of the
UI and the way we interact

00:24:38.930 --> 00:24:40.880
with computers overall.

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:43.490
GABI FERRARA: Does that mean
it should have been an email?

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:46.730
You probably are answering
that question with Voicea

00:24:46.730 --> 00:24:48.050
right now, right?

00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:52.160
Because all the meetings,
you'll get all of the stuff--

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:54.440
keywords, what is
important-- you

00:24:54.440 --> 00:24:58.740
send to the integration
systems for task management.

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:01.490
So in the end, you
have everything

00:25:01.490 --> 00:25:07.383
that you need just by having
your device or Eva helping you.

00:25:07.383 --> 00:25:09.800
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: I think it's
actually a very fair point.

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:12.320
In the future, I
wouldn't exclude us

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:14.180
from doing something
similar, to tell you,

00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:15.847
maybe you shouldn't
attend this meeting.

00:25:15.847 --> 00:25:16.960
MARK MANDEL: [LAUGHS]

00:25:16.960 --> 00:25:18.543
MOHAMED EL-GEISH:
As a matter of fact,

00:25:18.543 --> 00:25:20.620
we are working on a
lot of things that will

00:25:20.620 --> 00:25:22.010
help you make that decision.

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:25.510
One of it is that we have,
as humans, intrinsic Fear

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:27.550
Of Missing Out, or FOMO.

00:25:27.550 --> 00:25:29.950
But you don't actually have
to sit there for an hour

00:25:29.950 --> 00:25:34.130
and listen to the meeting if
you can send Eva on your behalf,

00:25:34.130 --> 00:25:36.020
and Eva will come
back reporting to you,

00:25:36.020 --> 00:25:37.650
here are the important minutes.

00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:40.750
You can just spend one
minute or a couple of minutes

00:25:40.750 --> 00:25:43.250
to listen to the important
minutes, or read them.

00:25:43.250 --> 00:25:45.970
And we worked on this
really amazing concept

00:25:45.970 --> 00:25:49.340
in collaboration, which
is teams and channels.

00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:52.000
So let's say I
manage a team, and we

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:54.050
have a meeting with a
client, and they want

00:25:54.050 --> 00:25:55.300
to share this meeting with me.

00:25:55.300 --> 00:25:57.230
So they can just
create that channel,

00:25:57.230 --> 00:25:59.830
and then I have access
to the channel that has

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:01.630
all of this client's meetings.

00:26:01.630 --> 00:26:03.370
And then I can coach
them through it,

00:26:03.370 --> 00:26:06.460
or I can follow up
with the customers

00:26:06.460 --> 00:26:08.510
if I needed to be
in that meeting

00:26:08.510 --> 00:26:10.010
and I couldn't make
it, for example,

00:26:10.010 --> 00:26:11.690
or I'm double-booked
or triple-booked.

00:26:11.690 --> 00:26:13.270
So FOMO is very important.

00:26:13.270 --> 00:26:17.140
And we want to satisfy
that itch by giving you,

00:26:17.140 --> 00:26:21.160
here's what's really happening
with this series of meetings

00:26:21.160 --> 00:26:22.670
in a very succinct way.

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:23.840
Here are the highlights.

00:26:23.840 --> 00:26:27.520
And I think you can
get a really high value

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:31.030
from just reading those
or following up on those

00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:33.160
instead of attending
all of these meetings.

00:26:33.160 --> 00:26:36.050
And I wouldn't be
surprised if we end up

00:26:36.050 --> 00:26:38.210
answering the question,
"should this meeting be

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:38.960
an email instead?"

00:26:38.960 --> 00:26:39.935
GABI FERRARA: [LAUGHS]

00:26:39.935 --> 00:26:41.560
MARK MANDEL: Or is
it going to be like,

00:26:41.560 --> 00:26:42.852
"don't have meetings with John.

00:26:42.852 --> 00:26:44.286
John tends to waffle."

00:26:44.286 --> 00:26:46.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:49.300
No, that sounds great.

00:26:49.300 --> 00:26:51.220
If people want to learn
more about Voicea,

00:26:51.220 --> 00:26:53.520
where should they go to
learn about this stuff?

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:56.800
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: So
differently www.Voicea.com.

00:26:56.800 --> 00:26:59.570
We're also on
LinkedIn, on Twitter.

00:26:59.570 --> 00:27:01.822
Google Voicea and
interact with us.

00:27:01.822 --> 00:27:03.280
I would love to
hear your feedback.

00:27:03.280 --> 00:27:05.020
And sign up today.

00:27:05.020 --> 00:27:08.680
It's free, and you get a trial
for our premium solution.

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:09.940
And I hope you enjoy it.

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:10.240
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:12.160
And if anyone wants to learn
about any of this stuff

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:13.430
you've talked about today--

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:14.680
it sounds like you're working
on some really interesting

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:17.013
problems-- do you have any
resources that you recommend?

00:27:17.013 --> 00:27:18.550
MOHAMED EL-GEISH:
Yes, so definitely

00:27:18.550 --> 00:27:21.050
feel free to reach out to us.

00:27:21.050 --> 00:27:22.760
We can get you a demo.

00:27:22.760 --> 00:27:24.790
And if you want to
reach out personally,

00:27:24.790 --> 00:27:27.480
I can also add my
contact information here.

00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:31.330
And anybody on the team, we have
an About page and Contact Us

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:32.592
page on Voicea.com.

00:27:32.592 --> 00:27:33.550
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:27:33.550 --> 00:27:35.425
Before we run away, is
there any other things

00:27:35.425 --> 00:27:38.008
that we haven't managed to touch
on that you want to make sure

00:27:38.008 --> 00:27:39.100
that people know about?

00:27:39.100 --> 00:27:41.500
MOHAMED EL-GEISH: I
think, as a parting note,

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:43.150
this is a very exciting era.

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:45.430
And I think folks--

00:27:45.430 --> 00:27:48.670
we've heard a lot of
things about AI taking

00:27:48.670 --> 00:27:51.670
over the world and AI being
this fourth revolution

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:53.260
after the Industrial
Revolution, how

00:27:53.260 --> 00:27:57.250
it's going to change the shape
and form of how we do work.

00:27:57.250 --> 00:27:59.350
I want to emphasize
the way we're

00:27:59.350 --> 00:28:02.290
doing things and
thinking about AI more

00:28:02.290 --> 00:28:04.090
of an augmented intelligence.

00:28:04.090 --> 00:28:05.980
So it's not here to
replace your job.

00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:08.800
Some jobs, over time,
will evolve and change.

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:10.820
And some of them are
going to disappear.

00:28:10.820 --> 00:28:12.910
But I would send the
message to everybody

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:15.700
to say, think of
AI as your friend,

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:19.270
and make sure that you know
more about what's happening,

00:28:19.270 --> 00:28:21.940
because it's going to affect
how we do work in the future.

00:28:21.940 --> 00:28:24.900
It's accelerated-- definitely on
an accelerated path right now.

00:28:24.900 --> 00:28:25.900
GABI FERRARA: Of course.

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:28.383
It's here to improve your
job, not take your job.

00:28:28.383 --> 00:28:29.800
MOHAMED EL-GEISH:
But befriend AI.

00:28:29.800 --> 00:28:31.240
GABI FERRARA:
Thank you, Mohamed.

00:28:31.240 --> 00:28:31.690
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:28:31.690 --> 00:28:32.830
All right, well,
Mohamed, thank you

00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:34.955
so much for hanging out
with us today, and chatting

00:28:34.955 --> 00:28:36.040
to us all about Voicea.

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:36.800
MOHAMED EL-GEISH:
Thank you so much.

00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:38.000
Thanks for having me.

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:39.750
GABI FERRARA: Thank
you, Mohamed El-Geish,

00:28:39.750 --> 00:28:42.670
for helping us understand more
about what Voicea is doing,

00:28:42.670 --> 00:28:45.340
and all the insights
that you gave on machine

00:28:45.340 --> 00:28:46.407
learning on this episode.

00:28:46.407 --> 00:28:47.990
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
super-cool episode.

00:28:47.990 --> 00:28:49.365
Thank you so much
for joining us.

00:28:49.365 --> 00:28:52.330
GABI FERRARA: So Mark, the
moment that you're waiting for,

00:28:52.330 --> 00:28:53.600
Question of the Week.

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:56.000
What if I'm working a
terminal in Cloud Shell

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:58.100
and I want to move
to another computer?

00:28:58.100 --> 00:28:59.503
How can I continue my work?

00:28:59.503 --> 00:29:01.420
MARK MANDEL: So that's
a really good question.

00:29:01.420 --> 00:29:02.590
And I found this
out the other day.

00:29:02.590 --> 00:29:03.530
I was really excited.

00:29:03.530 --> 00:29:06.457
So if you haven't used Cloud
Shell, it's kind of awesome.

00:29:06.457 --> 00:29:08.290
It's basically an
integrated terminal that's

00:29:08.290 --> 00:29:09.452
built into Cloud Console.

00:29:09.452 --> 00:29:11.410
It also has a matching
editor and a whole bunch

00:29:11.410 --> 00:29:12.620
of other fun things.

00:29:12.620 --> 00:29:13.995
So it means that
if you just want

00:29:13.995 --> 00:29:16.300
to get something done with
G Cloud or maybe kubectl,

00:29:16.300 --> 00:29:17.980
or something like that,
and a bunch of other tools

00:29:17.980 --> 00:29:19.960
that come with it as well,
it's just a really handy way

00:29:19.960 --> 00:29:22.060
of bringing up a terminal
and getting things done.

00:29:22.060 --> 00:29:24.070
What's super cool
about it is, not only

00:29:24.070 --> 00:29:25.540
do you get persistent
disk storage

00:29:25.540 --> 00:29:27.490
for a certain amount of time
as well, so you can leave stuff

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:27.990
in there.

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:29.913
And if you move from
computer to computer,

00:29:29.913 --> 00:29:32.080
as long as you're accessing
that on a regular basis,

00:29:32.080 --> 00:29:33.970
it stays there, which
is really great.

00:29:33.970 --> 00:29:35.050
What I didn't realize--

00:29:35.050 --> 00:29:36.640
and the implications
of which is--

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:38.140
it actually uses a
piece of software

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:39.430
called tmux in the background.

00:29:39.430 --> 00:29:40.972
And if you haven't
used that locally,

00:29:40.972 --> 00:29:42.910
it's basically a terminal
session management.

00:29:42.910 --> 00:29:45.670
It keeps those terminals
alive and persistent.

00:29:45.670 --> 00:29:47.928
So what happens is, say
you're working on one computer

00:29:47.928 --> 00:29:49.220
and you're like, that was cool.

00:29:49.220 --> 00:29:50.380
And then you go to
the office, and you're

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:52.520
working on your workstation,
for example, and you're like,

00:29:52.520 --> 00:29:54.130
oh, I wonder if there's still
a running process running,

00:29:54.130 --> 00:29:55.667
or I want to check
what's going on.

00:29:55.667 --> 00:29:58.000
You open up your terminal,
and Cloud Shell will be like,

00:29:58.000 --> 00:29:59.800
oh, hey, I transferred
your processes

00:29:59.800 --> 00:30:02.188
over from the old
computer to this one

00:30:02.188 --> 00:30:03.730
so you can still
see what's going on.

00:30:03.730 --> 00:30:04.670
And it's all the same.

00:30:04.670 --> 00:30:06.850
So for example, I was
doing a bunch of cleanup.

00:30:06.850 --> 00:30:09.545
I had a slew of App Engine
versions lying around.

00:30:09.545 --> 00:30:11.920
And so I'd written a script
to just delete a whole bunch.

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:13.870
It was going to take
about half an hour.

00:30:13.870 --> 00:30:16.840
And I had basically started it
at home, and then got on a bus,

00:30:16.840 --> 00:30:19.150
and gone to work, got to
work, and then fired up

00:30:19.150 --> 00:30:21.500
Cloud Shell on my workstation,
which was my laptop.

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:22.970
And it was like, oh, I
wonder if it's finished yet.

00:30:22.970 --> 00:30:25.060
And I popped it up, and I could
see it was still processing.

00:30:25.060 --> 00:30:26.340
And I was in a completely
different browser,

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:27.490
on a completely
different computer.

00:30:27.490 --> 00:30:28.970
And I was like, that is awesome.

00:30:28.970 --> 00:30:30.440
GABI FERRARA: Mind-blowing.

00:30:30.440 --> 00:30:34.690
There's this untold joke that
Cloud Shell is the best product

00:30:34.690 --> 00:30:35.695
that we don't advertise.

00:30:35.695 --> 00:30:38.020
MARK MANDEL: [LAUGHS]
I think that's fair.

00:30:38.020 --> 00:30:40.610
GABI FERRARA: So there is
a link on the podcast post

00:30:40.610 --> 00:30:41.350
with the feature.

00:30:41.350 --> 00:30:42.472
So take a look there.

00:30:42.472 --> 00:30:43.930
MARK MANDEL:
Absolutely, fantastic.

